<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We describe a process of growing trees from an amoeba, which is a tree <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> with a number assigned to each vertex. In a simplified language, this number <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(v)</annotation></semantics></math> tells how many extra edges we will require around a <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(H)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Given a tree <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> we look at a fixed copy <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> within <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and look whether every <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(H^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(H^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> is incident with at least <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(v)</annotation></semantics></math> edges within <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E(T)\setminus E(H^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(T)\setminus E(H^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
If not, we attach the corresponding number of pendent edges to <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>, increasing the size of <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.
A particularly interesting question is whether such growth process can stop or go on forever.
This has relations to several known models:</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">String and graph generation models</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">are intensively studied in theoretical computer science, formal language theory, and mathematical biology.
There are many models of this sort: cellular automata, rewriting systems, graph grammars etc. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. In particular,
our model resembles graph relabelling systems with forbidden contexts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Most of graph generation models use labelling of graphs. In contrast, we propose a simple rule of tree growth that governed by the structure of subtrees and does not use labels on vertices and/or edges. The resulting process is interesting from graph-theoretical point of view and possibly might impact the area of graph generation models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Galton-Watson process</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">is a foundational concept in the realm of probability theory, specifically in the study of family trees and the dynamics of progeny within populations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Introduced by Francis Galton and Henry Watson <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, this stochastic process serves as a mathematical model to examine the transmission of traits, characteristics, or lineage through successive generations of families <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. The key idea revolves around the probability distribution of offspring within a family, where individuals independently give rise to a random number of descendants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. This model has proven particularly valuable in fields such as genetics and demography <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, providing insights into the likelihood of lineage continuation or extinction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. The Galton-Watson process remains an essential tool for understanding the unpredictable nature of family structures and the variability in the number of progeny across generations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the probabilistic nature of the Galton-Watson process, our process represents a deterministic analog with a distinct feature—its replication criteria are not dictated by a random probability distribution. Instead, the expansion is orchestrated by the existence of a precisely chosen subgraph within the existing tree, acting as a deterministic trigger for the generation of additional leaves or progeny.
This deterministic analog introduces a structured and controlled element to the reproductive dynamics, offering a unique perspective on how specific configurations within a family lineage can influence and determine the proliferation of descendants. This departure from stochastic randomness to a deterministic criterion opens up new avenues for exploring the interplay between structure and growth of trees, providing a nuanced understanding of the factors that contribute to the perpetuation or divergence of lineages.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Avoidable paths</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">were studied in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. This concept was generalized further to the case of arbitrary two-rooted graphs in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> where the so-called <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">inherent graphs</em> were introduced.
Verifying inherence is a difficult task. For example, it is not known whether the path <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> with edges <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="st" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">st</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tv" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tv</annotation></semantics></math> is inherent provided <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> are the roots.
Interestingly, this problem is related to the cops and robbers game <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>.
The paper <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite> presents necessary conditions for inherence in terms of a <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pendant extension</em> process, which extends a two-rooted graph by pendant edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The process of growing trees from an amoeba
generalizes the pendant extension process.
It might be useful for further generalizations of avoidability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is structured as follows. In the beginning of <a href="#S2" title="2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> we introduce the main concepts of the paper, such as <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">amoeba</em>, its <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">extension</em>, <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">growth</em>, <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mortality</em> and <em id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">confinement</em>.
In <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Initial observations ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> we relate mortality and confinement conditions
while in <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Orbit lemma ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> we discuss relations between the symmetries of the underlying graphs and the amoeba mortality.
In <a href="#S3" title="3 Case of 1-extensions ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> we focus on the mortality conditions for amoebas growing in a simplest way, namely, by attaching pendant edges.
We identify degree conditions which are necessary for any such amoeba to be immortal in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Degree conditions ‣ 3 Case of 1-extensions ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, while in <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Mortality criteria for caterpillars ‣ 3 Case of 1-extensions ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> we describe the immortal amoebas whose underlying graph is a caterpillar.
We conclude the paper by <a href="#S4" title="4 Directions for future work ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, in which we propose some conjectures as well as directions for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Amoebas</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">An <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ordinary amoeba</em><span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
            
            
            
            
          This name was recently given to a different class of graphs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. We think that these two classes can not be confused.</span></span></span> <math id="S2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(T,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(T,m)</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as a pair consisting of a tree <math id="S2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, together with the
<em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">root function</em>
<math id="S2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m:V(T)\to\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m:V(T)\to\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> mapping every vertex <math id="S2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to a non-negative integer <math id="S2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(v)</annotation></semantics></math> called <em id="S2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiplicity</em> of <math id="S2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A vertex from <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of positive multiplicity is called a root of <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>.
Furthermore define <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k(A)=\sum_{v\in V(H)}m(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k(A)=\sum_{v\in V(H)}m(v)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">If not stated otherwise, the amoebas discussed in this paper will always be ordinary.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">An <em id="S2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-extension of amoeba</em> <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> is a graph <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> obtained from <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> by applying to each vertex <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> the following procedure: take <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m(v)</annotation></semantics></math> disjoint paths
<math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{\ell+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{\ell+1}</annotation></semantics></math>
and identify one endpoint of each with <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. The paths are called <em id="S2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">extension paths</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">A <em id="S2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">copy of amoeba</em> <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> in a graph <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is an amoeba <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(H^{\prime},m^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(H^{\prime},m^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{\prime}\leq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≤</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{\prime}\leq G</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\cong H^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>≅</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\cong H^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the functions <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m,m^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m,m^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> coincide under the corresponding isomorphism.
When no fear of ambiguity, we denote amoeba and its copy by the same notation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Let
<math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math>
be a copy of an amoeba in a tree <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Then a <em id="S2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth</em> <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>G</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is a tree
containing both <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and an <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-extension of <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and having the minimal possible number of edges.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">If, for a copy <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> of an amoeba in graph <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, all growths of <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> coincide with <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, then the copy is called <em id="S2.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">dead</em>. Otherwise, the copy is called <em id="S2.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">alive</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a copy of an amoeba may have non-isomorphic growths, see
example in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2401.07484v1/growth-10-mps.svg" id="S2.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="width:12.041em; height:6.413em;aspect-ratio:160/85;" width="160" height="85"></object></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2401.07484v1/growth-11-mps.svg" id="S2.F1.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_img_landscape" style="width:12.041em; height:6.413em;aspect-ratio:160/85;" width="160" height="85"></object></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Non-isomorhic 3-growths of the same copy of amoeba. The copy
is shadowed in grey, the new edge is dashed</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">For an amoeba <math id="S2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">growth sequence</em> of <math id="S2.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> starting at a tree <math id="S2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>
is a sequence of trees <math id="S2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0},T_{1},\dots" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0},T_{1},\dots</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="S2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0}=T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0}=T</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i+1}</annotation></semantics></math> is a growth of a copy of <math id="S2.p9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i+1}\neq T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i+1}\neq T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For an amoeba <math id="S2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math>, a <math id="S2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-<em id="S2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generation sequence</em> of <math id="S2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximal <math id="S2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth sequence starting at <math id="S2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p11.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> is clear from the context it will be omitted in notation for extensions, growths, generation sequences etc.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p12.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> be the extension parameter and <math id="S2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> be an amoeba.
It is <em id="S2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">immortal</em> if it admits an infinite generation sequence,
otherwise it is <em id="S2.p12.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mortal</em>.
Amoeba <math id="S2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p12.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">strongly immortal</em> if all its generation sequences are infinite. Amoeba <math id="S2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p12.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">partially immortal</em> if it admits an infinite growth sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p13.1" class="ltx_p">A tree <math id="S2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> is <em id="S2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">confining</em> for an amoeba <math id="S2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> if it admits a copy of <math id="S2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and all copies of <math id="S2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> are dead.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p14.1" class="ltx_p">An amoeba is <em id="S2.p14.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">totally confined</em> if for each tree <math id="S2.p14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> containing a copy of <math id="S2.p14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> there exists a confining tree <math id="S2.p14.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> containing a copy of <math id="S2.p14.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Confining trees can be replaced by confining
graphs.
Totally confined amoebas can be defined in this more general setting too.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, general two-rooted graphs are considered in the extension process.
Since attaching paths to a graph does not change its set of cycles, this process is finite for non-trees.
As we mainly study mortality, we restrict our attention to the case of trees.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Initial observations</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note that a copy of an amoeba may have non-isomorphic growths, which make things complicated. So, we start from study of this effect to show that it appears for small amoebas (with respect to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>) and in early members of growth sequences. An example in Fig. <a href="#S2.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can not be seen in generation sequences, as we will prove. It is caused by the minimality condition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth of a alive copy of an amoeba <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> in a tree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the corresponding extension of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> contained in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be a path <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0},v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0},v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> of the extension starting at a vertex <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{\ell-1},v_{\ell}\}\notin E(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{\ell-1},v_{\ell}\}\notin E(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Take a connected component of the forest <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}-H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>−</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}-H</annotation></semantics></math> containing <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is a tree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> rooted at <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is called an <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">area</em> of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to the minimality condition, the depth of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (the maximal length of a path from the root to a leaf) is <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> is a unique path of length <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math> from the root <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that other edges in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are the edges of the original tree <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. In this way we come to the following conlusion.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">An intersection of an area of an extension path and the original graph is a tree of depth at most <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Given a tree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and its subtree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, there is a partial order on <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V(T)\setminus V(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(T)\setminus V(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> defined as follows: <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="u\leq^{T^{\prime}}v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u\leq^{T^{\prime}}v</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> belongs to the path from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(T)\setminus V(T^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(T)\setminus V(T^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> the set <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{u:v\leq^{T^{\prime}}u\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msup><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{u:v\leq^{T^{\prime}}u\}</annotation></semantics></math> induces a tree <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>v</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> rooted at <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>.
We omit the mentioning of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>T</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> in the exponent when it is clear from context.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{0},T_{1},\dots,T_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{0},T_{1},\dots,T_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>-growth sequence for an amoeba <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v\in V(T_{k})\setminus V(T_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v\in V(T_{k})\setminus V(T_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> has the depth at most <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell-1</annotation></semantics></math>. If for some vertex <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>v</mi><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{I}}_{v}^{T_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> there are two different paths <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> of equal lengths from <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> to a leaf, then</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{{diam}}(H)\leq 2\ell-4\ \text{and}\ \text{{diam}}(T_{0})\leq 2\ell-6." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>diam</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">and</mtext><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>diam</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{{diam}}(H)\leq 2\ell-4\ \text{and}\ \text{{diam}}(T_{0})\leq 2\ell-6.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">W.l.o.g. we can assume that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> have no common edges.
Indeed, otherwise we can change the vertex <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> by the last common vertex of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Choose the maximal <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> such that at least one edge of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P\cup P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>∪</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\cup P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is not in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
W.l.o.g. we can assume that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=(u_{0},u_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=(u_{0},u_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is the last edge of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is not in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
It means that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is added in a growth in <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> of a copy <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(H,m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(H,m)</annotation></semantics></math> of the amoeba.
So an extension of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> contains an extension path from a root <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We claim that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>u</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u</annotation></semantics></math> is not on the path from <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> that has no common edges with <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Indeed, otherwise <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> does not intersect <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and the extension path can be changed to the path containing <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the minimality condition, see Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.1 Initial observations ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a).
Also, <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> is not on <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>. Indeed, otherwise the extension should add more edges, since the length of the whole path <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> is less than <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math>, see Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Proof. ‣ 2.1 Initial observations ‣ 2 Amoebas ‣ Growing Trees and Amoebas’ Replications" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b).</span></p>
</div>
